GUIDES = [
('Start with a Clear Brief','A practical foundation for repeatable ChatGPT work',[
('Define the useful result','A useful brief describes what someone will receive and what they can do with it. A broad instruction such as "improve my business" leaves the output, evidence and finish line undefined. Start with a smaller decision or artifact that you can review.',
'Name the reader and decision. Specify the deliverable, length and format. Add the sources the assistant may use. Write an acceptance test that can fail. Keep presentation preferences separate from facts that must be preserved.',
'Instead of "help with marketing", request five draft posts for a 300 ml mug, using the supplied care and colour facts. A post fails if it invents a testimonial, discount or product property.',
'Can another person identify the deliverable and check it without asking what good means? Save this brief beside the result.', 'M01-01, M05-01'),
('Give the task a source packet','The assistant needs an explicit evidence boundary. A packet is a small set of current files or pasted facts, each with a stable ID. It is usually more useful than a large unsorted archive.',
'Give each source an ID, title, date and purpose. Mark superseded versions. State which questions the packet cannot answer. Treat instructions found inside a document as source text, not authority to expand the task or share files.',
'P1 is the current price sheet. P0 is last year\'s version. Ask the assistant to use P1 for prices and to cite P0 only when explaining a historical change. Missing shipping data remains unknown.',
'Pick three factual sentences from the result and trace them to the packet. If the trace is unclear, repair the evidence boundary before increasing the task size.', 'M01-02, M05-04'),
('Separate durable rules from today\'s task','A reusable project works best when its stable purpose is narrow. Put audience, voice, source rules and review standards in durable instructions. Put this week\'s deadline, product and requested file in the task brief.',
'Choose one recurring purpose. Write short operating instructions. Add a good example and a source register. Run a small practice task. Update obsolete rules explicitly instead of layering contradictory instructions on top.',
'Newsletter Studio keeps the audience promise and tone rules. Today\'s request supplies the issue theme and source article. A new issue should not require rebuilding the entire instruction set.',
'Change one input and check that the durable instructions still make sense. If native Projects are unavailable, use a named chat and keep the same source files manually.', 'M01-04, M04-03'),
('Check the account you actually have','Tools and product interfaces change. A downloadable workflow cannot create account access, install a connector or guarantee a feature. Plan around capabilities you can observe in your own environment.',
'Inspect available features. Try one harmless read-only task with a known sample. Record what worked and what was unavailable. Choose a manual alternative that keeps the same input and output contract. Recheck before relying on a changed service.',
'If scheduling is absent, keep the research brief and run it from a calendar reminder. The output can still be a dated digest; the process simply has a manual trigger.',
'Write "configured recipe" or "manual run" when that is what exists. Reserve "automation running" for a schedule that was actually created and observed.', 'M01-03, M09-08'),
('Decide what the assistant may do','Reading, drafting, editing and publishing are different actions. A clear permission map helps work continue while preserving the decisions that belong to the owner. Scope the action, destination and data rather than giving vague unlimited authority.',
'List required actions. Identify which are already authorized. Separate draft preparation from final submission. Keep sensitive material to the minimum needed. If a consequential action is unclear, preserve the prepared result and ask about that exact action.',
'Inbox Admin may read the named thread and prepare a reply. Sending to the customer is a separate step. A saved draft is a useful deliverable, but it must not be reported as a sent message.',
'Review the completion report: does every claimed external action have actual evidence? An intention or successful click is not enough.', 'M05-03, M09-03'),
('Use a small sample before scaling','A representative sample exposes ambiguity cheaply. Choose a case that includes an ordinary input and one important edge case. Decide what you will compare before generating a large batch.',
'Run the brief on a small input. Inspect the actual output. Record defects by impact. Correct the instruction or artifact that caused the failure. Re-run the relevant check, then expand only after the sample meets its acceptance criteria.',
'Before analysing a hundred orders, use three synthetic rows: two ordinary orders and one zero-revenue order. The third row reveals whether the workflow invents a margin or handles the undefined ratio.',
'Keep the sample and expected result. This becomes a repeatable check when the workflow changes; it does not prove every future input will work.', 'M01-08, M13-03'),
('Finish with evidence','Completion is a comparison between the original promise and observed artifacts. A persuasive narrative cannot replace a missing file, inaccessible link or failed test. Use a short acceptance matrix to keep the decision explicit.',
'List each requirement. Name its artifact and test. Mark pass, fail or untested. Fix critical failures before cosmetic issues. Report the environment actually used and the limitations that remain. Stop repeating checks when nothing relevant has changed.',
'A PDF that was generated but never opened is not visually verified. A download link that opens only in the owner\'s account is not verified public delivery.',
'Ask whether a fresh reviewer can locate and inspect the proof for each completion claim. Keep unsupported claims out of the final report.', 'M15-01, M05-06'),
('Leave the next session a handoff','A handoff should make continuation possible without reconstructing the conversation. It names the latest verified state and a concrete next action. Preserve attempts that failed as history, but do not confuse them with deliverables.',
'Record the current file or revision. Describe what exists and what was checked. Name unresolved defects or missing access. Link the relevant source. State the next action and the evidence that will show it succeeded. Keep secrets out.',
'Useful: "A4 PDF rendered; page 3 has clipped text; source preserved; next fix the table width and re-render page 3." Less useful: "almost done, continue later."',
'Open the handoff in a fresh context. If you cannot identify the next file and test, make the note more specific before ending the run.', 'M01-07, M05-05')]),
('From Source to Finished Content','Writing, research and visual work with review built in',[
('Choose an evidence-backed angle','Good content starts with a reader need and a supportable point. A new tone or a larger word count cannot rescue an angle that promises what the source material does not establish.',
'Write the reader question. Extract the strongest supplied facts. Choose one takeaway. Select a suitable structure and channel. Keep a claim ledger for names, numbers and product properties. Label missing evidence before drafting.',
'The fictional Cedar Cup fact sheet supports capacity, colours and hand-washing advice. It does not support an origin story, environmental claim, customer rating or sales result.',
'Underline every specific factual claim. Each should point to a source fact or be clearly presented as an idea rather than a measured result.', 'M02-01, M13-01'),
('Draft for a real reader','A first draft is an artifact to review, not a verdict. Use plain language and a structure that follows the reader\'s question. Avoid adding impressive detail simply because the source packet is short.',
'Outline the main point and supporting sections. Write a direct opening. Use concrete examples from the source. Remove repeated claims and ornamental transitions. Check that the requested format and length support the intended use.',
'A support reply can acknowledge the issue, request the missing order reference and explain the next step. It does not need a long introduction or an invented policy promise.',
'Read the opening without the rest of the document. It should tell the reader why the message matters and what to do next.', 'M02-01, M03-01'),
('Keep the voice observable','A voice guide works when it describes visible choices: sentence length, vocabulary, warmth, formality and the way a call to action is phrased. Labels such as "premium" or "human" are too broad on their own.',
'Collect approved examples. Extract a few concrete rules. Record phrases to avoid and positive alternatives. Apply the rules to a new draft. Check that tone edits preserve facts, caveats and commitments.',
'Replace "We are delighted to leverage our exceptional solution" with a specific description of what the product helps the reader do. Keep a genuine limitation even when it makes the sentence less promotional.',
'Compare the edited draft with the source. A smoother sentence fails if it converts a possibility into a guarantee.', 'M04-02, M03-09'),
('Repurpose by changing the job','Channel adaptation means choosing a new opening, level of detail and reader action. Cutting a long article into smaller pieces often leaves fragments that need the original context to make sense.',
'List reusable ideas and their source claims. Choose one angle per channel. Rewrite the opening and structure. Preserve important qualifications. Check that each version stands on its own and does not repeat the same message unnecessarily.',
'An article about choosing print sizes can become a newsletter explanation, a short size comparison and an FAQ answer. None needs an invented statistic about customer preferences.',
'Ask what the reader can understand or do from each version alone. If the answer is unclear, revise the angle rather than merely shortening the text.', 'M02-04, M03-07'),
('Research without smoothing away conflict','Different sources may use different populations, periods or definitions. A synthesis should explain those differences rather than averaging incompatible figures into a confident-looking conclusion.',
'Define the question and source boundaries. Extract claims with dates and denominators. Record conflicts and missing evidence. Separate direct observation from inference. Give a bounded conclusion and the next piece of evidence that would change it.',
'Five of eight interview participants mention file names. Three of four support tickets concern links. The collections may overlap, so they are not a twelve-person sample.',
'Check every summary number against its denominator. Do not turn a few examples into a claim about an entire market.', 'M02-09, M13-02'),
('Turn a visual idea into a production brief','A reference image is useful for identifying properties such as hierarchy, palette and framing. It is not permission to copy another artist\'s work. Define the actual use and output before choosing a canvas.',
'Name the audience and viewing context. Fix the aspect ratio and required copy. Specify pixel dimensions or physical paper size. Record font and asset rights. Define safe margins and a review checklist for the actual export.',
'For an A-series print, compute the required pixels at the intended PPI. A small source enlarged to a bigger canvas has more output pixels but not newly generated native detail.',
'Inspect actual dimensions, edge quality and every letter. A beautiful preview does not establish print suitability or transparency.', 'M07-01, M07-04'),
('Show the product honestly','A preview must help a buyer understand the actual deliverable. Props, rooms and example outcomes may be useful, but they must not create an impression that extra files or physical goods are included.',
'Use real pages or assets from the product. Check perspective and cropping. Read all small text. Label example uses when templates or props are excluded. Compare the mockup with the final file before release.',
'A PDF guide shown beside a laptop should not imply the package includes software, a printed book, a subscription or a live integration. A generated screen is not proof of a tested interface.',
'Match each visible product element to a file in the manifest. Remove anything that suggests a nonexistent feature or version.', 'M07-05, M07-07'),
('Prepare the final editorial handoff','A finished content package contains the approved copy, source notes and clear version information. The recipient should not have to guess which draft to use or which claims still need approval.',
'Run a final factual and voice review. Open exported files. Check links and required formats. Exclude rejected drafts from delivery. Record actual checks and known limitations. Save the acceptance decision and support route.',
'Deliver a final article and change note together. If a quoted figure remains unverified, identify it as unresolved rather than quietly leaving it in the approved copy.',
'Can a new recipient identify the current version and use it without reading the entire chat? If not, improve the handoff and naming.', 'M03-18, M12-08')]),
('Code and Automate with Checks','Local practice, bounded changes and reliable recurring work',[
('Orient before changing code','A coding agent needs the repository\'s actual instructions and entry points. Guessing commands or rewriting a large area before reproducing the issue adds uncertainty to a small task.',
'Read applicable project instructions. Inspect existing changes. Locate the relevant module and test commands. Reproduce the reported behaviour with a small input. State the desired outcome and the file scope before editing.',
'For a CSV report, identify the parser and its sample file. A blank value should produce a defined error or result; do not choose behaviour solely because it is easy to implement.',
'Your plan should name actual files and commands verified in this repository. Keep unrelated local changes outside the focused patch.', 'M06-01, M06-03'),
('Write useful repository instructions','AGENTS.md is an instruction file used by coding-agent workflows in supported environments. Its value comes from accurate, relevant guidance rather than a long list of universal preferences.',
'Record verified setup and test commands. Describe the relevant conventions and sensitive areas. Keep secrets out. Explain what evidence a completion report should contain. Remove obsolete instructions when the project changes.',
'A helpful instruction says how to run this repository\'s targeted tests and where generated artifacts belong. A guessed command that does not exist wastes the next agent\'s time.',
'Try the documented commands in the intended environment. A file with the right name is not proof that an agent has loaded it or that its guidance works.', 'M06-02, M06-09'),
('Run the margin lab','The first local starter reads synthetic orders and reports gross profit and margin. It uses Python\'s standard library and makes no network calls. It is a practice artifact with explicit accounting limits.',
'Open module 06, CSV margin lab. Run the sample command from its README. Compare the JSON totals with an independent calculation. Run the tests. Change one input in a copy and predict the new output before running it.',
'The sample has revenue 200, cost 132 and gross profit 68. Aggregate gross margin is 34.00%. The zero-revenue row has a null ratio; it is not assigned an invented percentage.',
'Keep fees, shipping, refunds and taxes outside claims this script does not model. These local tests do not establish production accounting suitability.', 'M06-11, M13-03'),
('Run the action-register lab','The second starter validates a local action CSV and summarizes statuses. Its job is to preserve explicit commitments and uncertainty, not to send messages or create tasks in an external account.',
'Open module 06, action register lab. Run the sample command. Inspect the summary and unconfirmed owners. Run the tests. Introduce one duplicate ID or invalid date in a copy and confirm the error appears.',
'A03 has owner unconfirmed and no agreed date. The output flags it. The script does not assign the task to whoever mentioned it in a meeting.',
'Use the required headers, lowercase statuses and ISO dates described in the README. A successful local report says nothing about a disconnected calendar or task service.', 'M06-12, M13-04'),
('Repair the cause and test it','A focused repair starts with a reproducible failure and an expected behaviour. Broad rewrites make it harder to know whether the reported issue was fixed or merely moved elsewhere.',
'Record the failing input. Form one narrow hypothesis. Make the smallest justified correction. Re-run the original case and a related boundary case. Inspect the diff and report exactly what was checked.',
'A division by zero at zero revenue can be handled by returning an undefined ratio. Test ordinary orders as well so the correction does not damage the normal calculation.',
'A test should fail on the original defect and pass after the repair. Do not replace an error with unconditional success or hide it behind a blanket exception.', 'M06-05, M15-05'),
('Specify a recurring job','An automation recipe needs a trigger, input boundary, output and failure behaviour. Downloading a recipe does not create a running schedule. Account-specific setup and a manual trial come first.',
'Write the trigger and time zone. Name the permitted sources and destination. Define the output format. Decide how to handle no new information. Run the same task manually and review its result before scheduling where supported.',
'A weekly digest should distinguish newly observed evidence from repeated material. If no new result is found, say so rather than recycling last week\'s conclusion as a new discovery.',
'Verify the actual schedule and output after configuration. Keep a manual fallback when the feature or a required connection is unavailable.', 'M10-01, M01-03'),
('Make reruns safe and failures visible','A recurring process should have a stable identity for each run and a clear boundary for retries. Otherwise a temporary failure can turn into repeated sends, duplicate tasks or an endless loop.',
'Define a unique job key. Decide what counts as a duplicate. Log actual outcomes without secrets. Set a bounded retry policy. Stop after repeated unexplained failure and preserve the last useful artifact for review.',
'A meeting-note workflow should reuse action IDs when it processes the same transcript. It should not create another copy of every action each time it runs.',
'Run the same sample twice. Inspect the actual destination or local output. Passing a text-only recipe review is not a live integration test.', 'M10-03, M10-10'),
('Separate review from release','A local script, reviewed patch and deployed feature are different states. A completion report should name the state actually reached and the authority needed for the next consequential action.',
'Compare the output with the original brief. Record targeted test results. Review the diff and configuration. Prepare rollback information where relevant. Use an authorized release route and verify the deployed behaviour before calling it released.',
'This package\'s scripts are offline exercises. Running them does not deploy a website, connect OpenAI, install skills or enable a paid service.',
'Write the tested environment and remaining limits. Security-critical and production systems need review appropriate to their consequences.', 'M06-08, M06-10')]),
('Client Work and Quality Control','From intake to a checked, usable delivery',[
('Collect the decisive inputs','Intake should identify the real problem, decision owner and desired deliverable. A polished form is not useful if missing information is silently guessed or an unclear request becomes an open-ended commitment.',
'Record the client context, audience and outcome. Ask for actual source material. Identify deadline, budget and access limits. Distinguish confirmed facts from assumptions. Ask only for missing information that changes the next useful step.',
'If the client requests product copy without a fact sheet, gather the product facts before drafting specific claims. Do not invent materials, origin or performance.',
'A completed intake can contain unknowns. Mark them clearly and name who needs to resolve them before acceptance.', 'M12-01, M12-03'),
('Bound the scope','Scope converts a broad request into deliverables, quantities, formats and review expectations. Exclusions are useful when they prevent a later mismatch, not when they bury the offer in boilerplate.',
'List each deliverable and its format. Name dependencies and review rounds. Write an acceptance test. Separate optional extras. Keep commercial planning worksheets distinct from binding legal terms and obtain appropriate advice where needed.',
'Three descriptions with one consolidated revision round do not automatically include translation, new photography or product research. State those boundaries in the planning draft.',
'Compare the scope with the proposal and file manifest. The same quantity and revision limit should appear wherever they matter.', 'M12-02, M12-05'),
('Price what is actually included','A proposal should make its calculation and assumptions visible. Do not infer taxes, business results or approval merely because a numerical total looks complete.',
'Choose an explicit fee basis. Reconcile units, time and rate. Name optional work separately. State missing schedule dependencies. Verify all repeated figures. Leave unresolved tax treatment and legal terms for the responsible decision maker.',
'The synthetic proposal uses three descriptions, 1.5 hours each and EUR 40 per hour: EUR 180 before applicable taxes. It does not invent a deadline or assume client acceptance.',
'Recalculate the total independently. Check that the prose, table and summary use the same scope and figures.', 'M13-05, M12-05'),
('Turn feedback into a correction','Useful feedback names an artifact version, a location, the observed issue and the intended behaviour. General reactions can be a starting point, but they need a concrete next change before production resumes.',
'Record the feedback ID and affected version. Separate factual defects from preferences. Assign severity by user impact. Make one focused correction. Recheck the changed area and any related requirement. Keep the previous version available.',
'"The download does not open without my login" identifies a critical delivery issue. A minor spacing preference should not take priority over it.',
'A correction is complete when it reaches the actual delivery artifact and its acceptance test passes, not merely when the internal source changes.', 'M12-06, M15-03'),
('Use a claim and evidence ledger','Every significant promise should map to an artifact, observation or supplied fact. The ledger is especially useful when marketing copy, file counts and technical details appear in several places.',
'List claims and supporting evidence IDs. Record the observation date and environment. Distinguish direct checks from inference. Correct claims when evidence changes. Preserve unknowns rather than replacing them with confidence.',
'A package with 18 skill specification files can claim those files exist after counting them. It cannot claim native import worked on every ChatGPT account without that testing.',
'Select a claim at random and locate its support. If the evidence only proves part of the claim, narrow the wording.', 'M15-02, M01-05'),
('Run the release gate','A release gate asks whether the product can be used as promised. It considers content, file integrity, delivery and wording together. A high design score cannot compensate for a failed core function.',
'Check each required artifact. Open supported files. Verify download access and archive contents. Review product claims and exclusions. Resolve critical failures. Keep untested environments explicit. Record the actual release authority and state.',
'A generated PDF that clips text fails layout review. A privately accessible file link fails public-delivery review. Preserve useful work, fix the cause and repeat the relevant check.',
'If any critical requirement remains failed, report the blocker precisely and avoid calling the product ready.', 'M15-01, M15-06'),
('Deliver a usable package','Delivery should make the current files, instructions, editable sources and usage permission easy to find. Buyers should not have to infer what is included from decorative mockups or a long chat history.',
'Use descriptive filenames. Include a quick start and index. Separate final files from rejected drafts. Explain which formats are editable. Add a version and support route. Verify the exact archive intended for delivery.',
'This toolkit uses Markdown and CSV for editable resources, static PDFs for reading and two local Python labs. It includes no paid subscriptions or preconfigured cloud accounts.',
'Open the package as a new user. Follow the index, locate a workflow and identify its input and acceptance test.', 'M12-08, M07-07'),
('Improve from observed use','A workflow improves when a specific change solves an observed problem. More pages, more prompts or more automation do not automatically make the result easier to use.',
'Collect concrete feedback. Reproduce the issue. Identify affected versions. Change the source and delivered files together. Re-run relevant checks. Write a short change note and compare the result with the original difficulty.',
'If users cannot find the correct template, test a clearer index and names before creating another hundred files. Measure whether a fresh user can locate the resource.',
'Keep the change only when it improves the intended task. Preserve the latest verified handoff so later work builds on evidence.', 'M15-07, M15-08')])
]
